# auth/ holds the real login, logout, password-reset and OAuth-callback pages.
#
# This directory used to answer 404 to any request that did not arrive through an
# internal rewrite, so that /auth/login.php could never become a third URL for the
# login page alongside /login and /login.php. That was a URL-tidiness rule, and it
# was enforced in a file that only Apache reads.
#
# It had to go, because the application's own code has to be able to name the real
# location of its own login page. Every redirect in the app now points at
# `auth/login.php` (issue #68): a path that resolves on any web server, with no
# rewriting of any kind. Blocking it here would have turned every one of those
# redirects into a 404 on Apache — the same bug, in the opposite direction.
#
# The reason behind the old rule is dealt with at source instead: the pages in here
# no longer use relative asset paths, so they render identically whether they are
# reached as /login or as /auth/login.php. See the Root-Folder-Tidy wiki page.
#
# /login remains the canonical, prettier URL and is what people are shown; the root
# .htaccess still maps it here, and still 301s /login.php for old bookmarks.
#
# What must NOT be reachable from here is anything that is not a page. There are no
# includes or configuration files in auth/, and this makes sure it stays that way.
<FilesMatch "\.(inc|ini|log|sql|bak|orig|swp|dist|sample)$">
    <IfModule mod_authz_core.c>
        Require all denied
    </IfModule>
    <IfModule !mod_authz_core.c>
        Order allow,deny
        Deny from all
    </IfModule>
</FilesMatch>
